**Setting up registers and always block**

Next, I’m defining some registers:

```
reg h_csub_pending;
reg [`RISCV_FORMAL_XLEN-1:0] h_csub_rs1;
reg [`RISCV_FORMAL_XLEN-1:0] h_csub_rs2;
reg [`RISCV_FORMAL_XLEN-1:0] h_csub_res;
```

Then, I’ll create the always block:

```
always @(posedge clock) begin
    if (reset) begin
        h_csub_pending <= 0;
        h_csub_rs1 <= 0;
        h_csub_rs2 <= 0;
        h_csub_res <= 0;
    end else begin
        if (wrapper.uut.cpuregs_write && h_is_csub) begin
            h_csub_pending <= 1;
            h_csub_rs1 <= wrapper.uut.dbg_rs1val;
            h_csub_rs2 <= wrapper.uut.dbg_rs2val;
            h_csub_res <= wrapper.uut.dbg_rs1val - wrapper.uut.dbg_rs2val;
        end

        if (h_fire) begin
            h_csub_pending_set: assert(h_csub_pending);
            h_csub_rs1_consistent: assert(rvfi_rs1_rdata == h_csub_rs1);
            h_csub_rs2_consistent: assert(rvfi_rs2_rdata == h_csub_rs2);
            h_csub_res_consistent: assert(rvfi_rd_wdata == h_csub_res);
            h_csub_pending <= 0;
        end
    end
end
```

I’ve realized that using `h_csub_pending` for the assert label conflicts with the reg name, so I’ll opt for `h_csub_pending_set`. Since `cpuregs_write` is a combinational wire, it captures the high state at the time of the posedge. I wonder if this assertion will be inductive.

